## SECTION 1 â€” Executive Summary

KnowledgeIQ is a genuinely impressive solo-built enterprise RAG platform. The architecture demonstrates thoughtful design: microservice separation via gRPC, modular prompt templates, provider abstraction, trust scoring, duplicate/contradiction detection, category-aware retrieval, and structured logging. These are patterns I would expect from an experienced team, not a single developer.

However, several architectural decisions are blocking production readiness and are the direct cause of the reasoning leakage problem. The issues are not superficial â€” they are structural, and prompt engineering alone cannot fix them.

### Scorecard

| Dimension | Score | Notes |
|-----------|-------|-------|
| **Architecture** | 7/10 | Clean microservice split. gRPC boundaries well-defined. Some responsibility leakage between gateway and LLM service. |
| **Code Organization** | 7/10 | Logical folder structure. Some dead code and dual prompt files. Provider abstraction is good. |
| **Retrieval** | 6/10 | Functional but under-engineered. No reranking model. Hardcoded top-5 with naÃ¯ve diversity. No hybrid search. |
